Data: SDD

Purpose:
  Define data architecture patterns, database selection rules, data
  modeling guidelines, migration strategies, consistency models,
  partitioning approaches, retention policies, backup rules, and
  encryption requirements. Data architecture is selected based on
  access patterns, consistency requirements, and scale.

Owns: ./

Scope:
  Database selection (SQL vs NoSQL)
  Data modeling
  Migrations
  Data consistency (strong vs eventual)
  Data partitioning
  Data retention
  Data backup
  Data encryption

Principles:
  Choose database based on data characteristics, not familiarity.
  Schema design MUST match access patterns.
  Data MUST be owned by exactly one domain.
  Migrations MUST be backward-compatible.
  Encryption MUST be applied by default.

DatabaseSelection:

  SQL:
    description: Relational database with ACID transactions
    strengths:
      - strong consistency
      - complex queries and joins
      - mature ecosystem
      - ACID transactions
    weaknesses:
      - scaling challenges at high write volume
      - rigid schema
      - expensive at massive scale
    when_to_use:
      - financial transactions
      - complex relationships
      - strong consistency required
      - reporting with complex queries
    examples:
      - PostgreSQL
      - MySQL
      - SQL Server

  NoSQL:
    description: Non-relational database optimized for specific access patterns
    types:
      document:
        description: JSON-like documents
        examples: MongoDB, Couchbase
        when_to_use:
          - flexible schema
          - hierarchical data
          - rapid iteration

      key_value:
        description: Simple key-value pairs
        examples: Redis, DynamoDB
        when_to_use:
          - session storage
          - caching
          - high throughput simple lookups

      column_family:
        description: Wide-column stores
        examples: Cassandra, Bigtable
        when_to_use:
          - time-series data
          - high write throughput
          - massive scale
          - eventual consistency acceptable

      graph:
        description: Nodes and relationships
        examples: Neo4j, Neptune
        when_to_use:
          - highly connected data
          - relationship-heavy queries
          - social networks
          - recommendation engines

    when_to_use_general:
      - massive scale
      - flexible schema
      - specific access patterns
      - eventual consistency acceptable

  GraphModel:
    description: .sdd internal knowledge graph for entities and relationships
    purpose: semantic queries, impact analysis, context retrieval
    storage: logical graph over PostgreSQL
    navigation: @../graph/model.sdd
    rule: filesystem is for navigation, graph is for semantics

  HybridModel:
    description: SDDRA uses three complementary storage layers
    layers:
      - filesystem: git-friendly, human-editable .sdd representations
      - postgresql: ACID transactions, versioning, audit, constraints
      - graph_abstraction: semantic queries, traversal, impact analysis
    rule: physical storage and domain model are decoupled
    rule: graph is logical model, PostgreSQL is physical storage

  EntityIdentity:
    rule: every entity MUST have immutable ID
    rule: entity identity is separate from version
    rule: TASK-100 v1, v2, v3 are the same entity

  RelationshipVersioning:
    rule: relationship changes MUST be versioned
    rule: historical graph queries MUST be supported
    rule: graph snapshots MUST be created for executions

  SelectionCriteria:
    consistency_requirement: strong -> SQL, eventual -> NoSQL candidate
    query_complexity: complex joins -> SQL, simple lookups -> NoSQL
    schema_stability: stable -> SQL, evolving -> NoSQL candidate
    scale: moderate -> SQL, massive -> NoSQL candidate
    transaction_volume: high ACID -> SQL, high throughput -> NoSQL candidate

DataModeling:

  EntityRelationship:
    description: Define entities, attributes, and relationships
    rule: every entity MUST have a single owner domain
    rule: relationships MUST use explicit foreign keys or references

  SchemaDesign:
    normalization:
      - normalize to 3NF for OLTP systems
      - denormalize for read-optimized reporting
      - avoid over-normalization that hurts performance

    indexing:
      - index foreign keys
      - index frequently queried columns
      - avoid over-indexing (write penalty)
      - composite indexes for common query patterns

  AntiPatterns:
    sparse_columns:
      description: Many nullable columns
      rule: consider vertical partitioning or separate table

    entity_attribute_value:
      description: EAV pattern for schema flexibility
      rule: avoid unless truly dynamic schema required

    json_everything:
      description: Storing all data as JSON in SQL
      rule: use structured columns for queryable data

Migrations:

  principles:
    backward_compatible:
      - new columns MUST be nullable or have defaults
      - removed columns MUST not be referenced before removal
      - renamed columns MUST support old name temporarily

    atomic:
      - each migration MUST be a single atomic operation
      - no multi-step migrations that leave DB in inconsistent state

    reversible:
      - every migration MUST have a rollback path
      - test rollback before production deploy

    zero_downtime:
      - long-running migrations MUST use expand/contract pattern
      - background migration for large tables

  process:
    - migration reviewed in PR
    - migration tested on staging copy
    - migration applied during deploy window
    - post-migration verification

DataConsistency:

  StrongConsistency:
    description: All reads return the latest written value
    guarantee: linearizability
    cost: higher latency, lower availability
    use_when:
      - financial transactions
      - inventory management
      - any domain where stale data causes errors
    implementation:
      - synchronous replication
      - distributed locks
      - consensus protocols (Raft, Paxos)

  EventualConsistency:
    description: Reads may return stale values temporarily
    guarantee: convergence
    cost: lower latency, higher availability
    use_when:
      - social feeds
      - analytics
      - non-critical user data
      - cache layers
    implementation:
      - asynchronous replication
      - conflict resolution on read
      - version vectors

  Selection:
    rule: default to strong consistency
    rule: eventual consistency MUST be explicitly justified
    rule: eventual consistency boundaries MUST be documented

DataPartitioning:

  VerticalPartitioning:
    description: Split table by columns
    when_to_use:
      - wide tables with hot/cold columns
      - separating PII from business data
      - access pattern separation

  HorizontalPartitioning:
    description: Split table by rows
    strategies:
      range: date ranges, ID ranges
      hash: hash of key modulo N
      list: discrete values (tenant_id, region)
    when_to_use:
      - large tables (100M+ rows)
      - time-series data
      - multi-tenant isolation

  Selection:
    rule: partition by most common query filter
    rule: avoid cross-partition queries
    rule: consider partition key immutability

DataRetention:

  policy:
    - every data type MUST have defined retention period
    - retention MUST comply with regulatory requirements
    - retention MUST be enforced automatically

  strategies:
    archive:
      - move old data to cheaper storage
      - keep searchable but slower access
      - applicable: logs, historical transactions

    delete:
      - permanently remove after retention period
      - applicable: temporary data, sessions

    anonymize:
      - remove PII but keep structure
      - applicable: analytics, ML training data

DataBackup:

  frequency:
    - critical data: continuous replication
    - important data: hourly incremental
    - standard data: daily full + hourly incremental

  storage:
    - off-site storage (different region/cloud)
    - immutable backups (WORM storage)
    - encrypted at rest and in transit

  testing:
    - restore test quarterly minimum
    - measure restoration time against RTO
    - validate backup completeness

  anti_patterns:
    untested_backups:
      description: Backups never restored
      rule: MUST test restoration

    single_location:
      description: Backups in same location as primary
      rule: MUST store off-site

    unencrypted_backups:
      description: Backups without encryption
      rule: MUST encrypt all backups

DataEncryption:

  at_rest:
    - database storage encryption
    - backup encryption
    - file storage encryption
    - key management: use KMS, not application code

  in_transit:
    - TLS for all connections
    - certificate validation required
    - no plaintext protocols

  application:
    - sensitive fields encrypted at application layer
    - key rotation policy
    - separate encryption keys per data classification

  key_management:
    - use managed KMS when possible
    - key rotation: annually minimum
    - access to keys: strictly controlled
    - never store keys in source code or config files

AntiPatterns:

  DatabasePerDeveloper:
    description: Each developer uses own DB instance
    rule: shared development DB with migration-managed schema

  NoMigrations:
    description: Schema changes applied manually
    rule: ALL schema changes MUST use versioned migrations

  SharedDatabase:
    description: Multiple services share one database
    rule: each domain MUST own its data store

  OverIndexing:
    description: Index on every column
    rule: index based on actual query patterns

  NoRetentionPolicy:
    description: Data grows without bounds
    rule: every data store MUST have retention policy

  UnencryptedSensitiveData:
    description: PII or sensitive data stored plaintext
    rule: sensitive data MUST be encrypted at rest and in transit

  PrematureSharding:
    description: Sharding before exhausting simpler options
    rule: MUST optimize, add replicas, partition before sharding

  InconsistentNaming:
    description: Inconsistent column/table naming
    rule: follow established naming convention

Navigation:
  Levels: @levels.sdd
  Patterns: @patterns.sdd
  Scaling: @scaling.sdd
  Availability: @availability.sdd
  Observability: @observability.sdd
  Networking: @networking.sdd
  Deployment: @deployment.sdd
  Project: @project/project.sdd
  ProjectArchitecture: @projects/{project_name}/architecture/INDEX.sdd
  Decisions: @projects/{project_name}/decisions/INDEX.sdd

State: +
  